--- DESCRIPTION ---
How the male and female reproductive systems work together to achieve fertilisation and begin a pregnancy.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Female Reproductive System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The female reproductive system is designed to produce eggs, receive sperm, provide an environment for fertilisation, and support a developing baby throughout pregnancy. The main structures are the <dfn class="term" data-def="Two small almond-shaped organs in the female pelvis that produce and release eggs (ova) and the hormones oestrogen and progesterone.">ovaries</dfn>, the fallopian tubes, the uterus and the cervix. Each ovary contains hundreds of thousands of immature eggs from birth. Once puberty begins, hormones trigger a monthly cycle in which one egg matures and is released.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A hollow, pear-shaped muscular organ in the female pelvis where a fertilised egg implants and a baby develops during pregnancy.">uterus</dfn> (womb) is a thick-walled muscular organ about the size of a closed fist. Its inner lining, the endometrium, thickens each month in preparation for a possible pregnancy. If no fertilised egg arrives, this lining is shed during menstruation. The cervix is the narrow lower entrance to the uterus; it is normally kept closed by a mucus plug but softens and dilates during labour.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Draw a simple outline of the female reproductive system and label: ovaries, fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, vagina. Redraw from memory until you can place every structure correctly.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>A woman is born with all the eggs she will ever have — approximately one to two million immature eggs (follicles) at birth, falling to around 300,000 by puberty. Only about 400 of these will ever be released (ovulated) during her reproductive lifetime.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">The Menstrual Cycle</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A monthly hormonal cycle lasting approximately 28 days in which the uterine lining thickens, an egg is released at ovulation, and the lining is shed if fertilisation does not occur.">menstrual cycle</dfn> averages 28 days but varies between 21 and 35 days in different individuals. It is controlled by four hormones: follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) triggers an egg to mature inside a follicle in the ovary; oestrogen causes the uterine lining to thicken; luteinising hormone (LH) triggers ovulation (the release of the mature egg); and progesterone maintains the thickened lining after ovulation. If fertilisation does not occur, progesterone and oestrogen levels fall, the lining breaks down, and menstruation (a period) begins.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Ovulation typically occurs around day 14 of a 28-day cycle. The released egg travels along the fallopian tube towards the uterus and remains viable for fertilisation for approximately 12 to 24 hours. Sperm, by contrast, can survive in the female reproductive tract for up to five days, which means the fertile window around ovulation spans roughly six days.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">The Male Reproductive System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The male reproductive system is responsible for producing, storing and delivering sperm. The <dfn class="term" data-def="Two oval-shaped organs in the scrotum that produce sperm and the hormone testosterone.">testes</dfn> are located in the scrotum, slightly outside the body cavity, because sperm production requires a temperature about 2°C lower than normal body temperature. Sperm are produced continuously from puberty, with around 1,500 sperm made every second in a healthy adult male. Each sperm cell carries genetic information from the father and has a head containing the nucleus, a midpiece packed with mitochondria for energy, and a tail (flagellum) for propulsion.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Mature sperm are stored in the epididymis before passing through the vas deferens. During ejaculation, sperm mix with fluids from the seminal vesicles and prostate gland to form semen. This fluid nourishes sperm and creates a slightly alkaline environment that protects them from the acidity of the vagina. A single ejaculation typically contains 200 to 500 million sperm, although only a few hundred will reach the fallopian tubes.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What happens at fertilisation?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Fertilisation occurs when a single sperm successfully penetrates the outer layer of the egg (the zona pellucida) and fuses with it. This normally takes place in the outer third of the fallopian tube. At the moment of fertilisation, the egg completes its final division, and the genetic material from the sperm (23 chromosomes) combines with the genetic material of the egg (23 chromosomes) to form a zygote containing the full complement of 46 chromosomes. The sex of the embryo is determined at this point: if the sperm carries an X chromosome the baby will be female (XX); if it carries a Y chromosome the baby will be male (XY).</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">From Zygote to Implantation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">After fertilisation, the zygote immediately begins to divide by a process called cleavage. Over the next three to four days it travels along the fallopian tube, dividing repeatedly to form a morula (a solid ball of cells) and then a blastocyst (a hollow ball with a fluid-filled cavity). By around day 6 to 10 after fertilisation, the blastocyst reaches the uterus and burrows into the endometrium in a process called implantation. Successful implantation triggers the release of human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG), the hormone detected by home pregnancy tests, which prevents the uterine lining from shedding and signals to the body that pregnancy has begun.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Learn the correct sequence: fertilisation (day 0, fallopian tube) → cell division → blastocyst → implantation (days 6–10, uterine wall). Examiners commonly ask you to sequence these stages.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The sex of a baby is determined at fertilisation by the type of sperm that fertilises the egg. A sperm carrying a Y chromosome produces a male (XY) embryo; a sperm carrying an X chromosome produces a female (XX) embryo. The egg always contributes an X chromosome.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Mistake: Confusing ovulation and fertilisation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Ovulation is the release of an egg from the ovary &mdash; it does not mean conception has occurred. Fertilisation is when a sperm actually fuses with the egg. These are two separate events. Similarly, do not confuse implantation (the blastocyst embedding in the uterine wall) with fertilisation. A pregnancy is only established once the fertilised egg has successfully implanted. In exam answers, always use the correct term for the right stage.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n17">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">The female reproductive system produces eggs monthly through the menstrual cycle, with ovulation occurring around day 14; the male reproductive system continuously produces sperm which are ejaculated in semen.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Fertilisation occurs in the fallopian tube when a single sperm fuses with an egg, creating a zygote with 46 chromosomes; the sex of the embryo is determined at this moment by whether the sperm carries an X or Y chromosome.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">After fertilisation, the zygote divides and travels to the uterus, where it implants in the endometrium around days 6 to 10, establishing pregnancy and triggering hCG production.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n16">When questions ask you to &lsquo;describe the role&rsquo; of a reproductive structure, name the structure, state what it does and link it to the next stage in the process. For example: &lsquo;The fallopian tube transports the released egg towards the uterus and is the site where fertilisation occurs.&rsquo; Answers that just say &lsquo;it carries the egg&rsquo; without linking to fertilisation or implantation rarely score full marks. For sequence questions, learn the exact order: ovulation &rarr; fertilisation (in the fallopian tube) &rarr; cell division &rarr; implantation (in the uterine wall).</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
