=== L7 Postnatal Checks for the Newborn ===
id: 2b576a63-761d-45ea-ae52-1225789ebded
slug: lesson-07
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:26:19.62037+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 16

--- DESCRIPTION ---
The routine checks carried out on a newborn baby and the role of the Special Care Baby Unit for premature or ill.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Apgar Score</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Within one minute of birth, and again at five minutes, every newborn in the UK is assessed using the <dfn class="term" data-def="A scoring system used at 1 and 5 minutes after birth to assess a newborn's physical condition across five criteria, each scored 0–2, giving a maximum total of 10.">Apgar score</dfn>. The five criteria are: Appearance (skin colour), Pulse (heart rate), Grimace (reflex response to stimulation), Activity (muscle tone), and Respiration (breathing). Each is scored 0, 1 or 2, giving a maximum total of 10. A score of 7 or above indicates the baby is in good condition. A score of 4–6 suggests the baby may need some assistance, and a score below 4 indicates the baby requires immediate medical intervention. The Apgar score allows midwives and paediatricians to make rapid, objective decisions about whether a newborn needs urgent support.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Learn the five Apgar criteria using the mnemonic APGAR itself: Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration. Practise writing the score range and what each band means — examiners expect precise answers, not vague descriptions.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The Apgar score is assessed at 1 minute and 5 minutes after birth. A total of 7–10 indicates good condition; 4–6 may need assistance; below 4 requires immediate medical intervention. Maximum score is 10 (2 for each of the five criteria).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Weight, Head Circumference and Physical Examination</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">All newborns are weighed at birth and their <dfn class="term" data-def="The measurement around the widest part of the baby's head, taken shortly after birth to check brain development and detect abnormalities such as hydrocephalus.">head circumference</dfn> is measured. The average birth weight in the UK is approximately 3.3 kg, but a range of 2.5–4.5 kg is considered healthy. Weight below 2.5 kg classifies the baby as low birth weight, which is associated with prematurity or poor growth in the womb. Head circumference is typically 33–35 cm at term. An unusually small or large head may indicate conditions such as microcephaly or hydrocephalus respectively, and will prompt further investigation.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n6">Within 72 hours of birth, a qualified midwife or paediatrician carries out a comprehensive physical examination. This covers: eyes (checked for cataracts and structural abnormalities using an ophthalmoscope), mouth (checked for cleft palate), heart (listened to for murmurs), lungs, abdomen (to detect enlarged organs), spine (checked for spina bifida), hips (Ortolani and Barlow manoeuvres to detect developmental dysplasia of the hip), feet (checked for talipes/club foot), genitalia, reflexes (Moro, grasp, rooting), and skin condition. This examination provides a baseline record of the baby's health and flags issues that need follow-up.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The Newborn Blood-Spot (Heel-Prick) Test</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n7">Between 5 and 8 days after birth, a midwife takes a small sample of blood from a prick on the baby's heel. This is absorbed onto a card and sent to a laboratory where it is screened for ten rare but serious conditions: phenylketonuria (PKU), congenital hypothyroidism, cystic fibrosis, sickle cell disease, MCADD (medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency), maple syrup urine disease, isovaleric acidaemia, glutaric aciduria type 1, homocystinuria, and hereditary tyrosinaemia type 1 (HT1, added to the programme in 2025). These conditions are not visible at birth but can cause severe disability or death if untreated. Early detection allows treatment to start before symptoms develop, significantly improving long-term outcomes. The test is part of the NHS Newborn Blood Spot Screening Programme and is offered to all newborns.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">The Newborn Hearing Test</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A non-invasive hearing screening test offered to all newborns, typically in hospital or at a home visit, that measures the ear's response to quiet sounds using either AABR or OAE technology.">newborn hearing test</dfn> is offered to all babies within the first few weeks of life, ideally before leaving hospital. It uses one or both of two technologies: the otoacoustic emission (OAE) test, which plays a soft clicking sound and measures the echo from the cochlea, and the automated auditory brainstem response (AABR) test, which uses small sensors on the head to measure brainwave responses to sounds. The test is painless and usually performed while the baby is calm or sleeping. Around 1 in 1,000 babies is identified with permanent hearing loss; early identification enables earlier intervention with hearing aids or cochlear implants, and access to language support, which significantly improves communication development.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="When explaining why each check is important, always link the check to the outcome it enables — e.g. the heel-prick detects PKU early so a diet can start before brain damage occurs. The 'why it matters' is what secures the higher marks.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The newborn blood-spot test screens for ten conditions including phenylketonuria (PKU), congenital hypothyroidism, cystic fibrosis and hereditary tyrosinaemia type 1 (HT1, added 2025). It is carried out between 5 and 8 days after birth by pricking the baby's heel. Early detection allows treatment before symptoms and irreversible harm develop.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The Special Care Baby Unit (SCBU)</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">The Special Care Baby Unit (SCBU) is a specialist ward within a hospital that provides intensive medical care for newborns who are premature (born before 37 weeks), have a low birth weight (below 2.5 kg), or are ill following birth. Premature babies have underdeveloped lungs, poor temperature regulation, difficulty feeding, and a weakened immune system. SCBU staff include neonatologists, specialist nurses and physiotherapists who provide incubator care to maintain warmth, oxygen therapy and ventilation support, tube feeding (nasogastric or intravenous), infection monitoring, and phototherapy for jaundice. Parents are encouraged to visit as often as possible, and kangaroo care (skin-to-skin contact) is actively promoted, as it stabilises the baby's heart rate and temperature and supports bonding and breastfeeding. The length of stay depends on the baby's gestation and health; some babies remain in SCBU for several months.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n13">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n14">The Apgar score assesses five criteria (Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration) at 1 and 5 minutes after birth; a score of 7–10 indicates good condition and below 4 requires immediate intervention.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n15">The newborn blood-spot test (heel prick at day 5–8) screens for ten serious conditions (including PKU, congenital hypothyroidism and HT1), and the hearing test identifies permanent hearing loss early — both enabling intervention before irreversible harm.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n16">The Special Care Baby Unit provides specialist care for premature or ill newborns through incubators, oxygen, tube feeding and phototherapy, with parental involvement actively encouraged.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n12">Questions on postnatal checks often ask you to 'explain the purpose' of a specific test. Always name the test, state what it checks for, and explain what would happen as a result of detection — for example, 'the newborn blood-spot test screens for phenylketonuria so that a low-phenylalanine diet can be introduced before brain damage occurs.' A three-part answer (name, detects, enables) will access the upper mark bands.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
