=== L9 Childhood Illnesses & Immunisation ===
id: 84461ba6-6ce6-408a-bc39-b45eabd4bad5
slug: lesson-09
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:39:42.369019+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 15

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Common childhood illnesses, when to seek medical help, the immunisation schedule, and the benefits and concerns.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Common Childhood Illnesses</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">All children will experience illness at some point, and parents and carers need to recognise common conditions and their symptoms. <dfn class="term" data-def="A highly contagious viral infection causing an itchy rash of fluid-filled blisters that crust over; caused by the varicella-zoster virus.">Chickenpox</dfn> is one of the most widespread childhood illnesses, spreading easily through airborne droplets or contact with the fluid in blisters. It begins with a fever and general malaise, followed within 1–2 days by an itchy rash that starts on the chest or back and spreads. The spots develop into blisters, then crust over. It is most contagious from two days before the rash appears until all spots have crusted. Children should be kept away from pregnant women and people with weakened immune systems. Most cases are mild and self-limiting; calamine lotion, cool baths and antihistamines ease itching.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n3"><dfn class="term" data-def="A serious viral infection causing a distinctive red blotchy rash, high fever, cough, runny nose and sore, light-sensitive eyes (conjunctivitis); a notifiable disease in the UK.">Measles</dfn> begins with fever, cough, runny nose and red, watery eyes (Koplik spots — white spots inside the cheeks — appear 2–3 days before the rash). The characteristic red blotchy rash spreads from the face down the body about four days into the illness. Measles is highly contagious and can lead to serious complications including ear infection, pneumonia and, rarely, encephalitis. It is a notifiable disease. Mumps causes swelling of the parotid (salivary) glands on one or both sides of the face, giving a 'hamster cheek' appearance, alongside fever and headache. It can lead to orchitis (testicular swelling) in post-pubertal males and, rarely, meningitis. Tonsillitis is an infection of the tonsils causing severe sore throat, pain on swallowing, fever, swollen cervical lymph nodes and white patches on the tonsils. It may be viral or bacterial; bacterial tonsillitis caused by Streptococcus requires antibiotics.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Meningitis warning signs are a common exam focus. Learn the non-blanching rash, stiff neck, high fever, severe headache and aversion to light. The glass test is a classic exam example — if the rash does not fade when a glass is pressed firmly against it, call 999 immediately.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Meningitis warning signs in children include: a stiff neck, high fever, severe headache, photophobia (dislike of bright light), vomiting, confusion or drowsiness, and a non-blanching rash (spots that do not fade when a glass is pressed firmly against the skin). A non-blanching rash indicates possible septicaemia — call 999 immediately.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Meningitis and When to Seek Medical Help</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="Inflammation of the meninges (the membranes surrounding the brain and spinal cord), which can be caused by bacteria or viruses; bacterial meningitis is a medical emergency.">Meningitis</dfn> is one of the most serious childhood illnesses. Bacterial meningitis, most often caused by Neisseria meningitidis (meningococcal) or Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcal), can progress from first symptoms to death within hours. The early signs mimic a flu-like illness, making it easy to miss. As the disease progresses, a distinctive non-blanching petechial or purpuric rash may appear — caused by bleeding under the skin from septicaemia. Parents should be advised to use the glass test (press a clear glass firmly over the rash: if it does not fade, it may be meningococcal disease) and call 999 immediately. Not all meningitis cases produce a rash; the combination of high fever, severe headache, stiff neck and photophobia should prompt urgent medical review regardless.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n7">More generally, parents should seek medical attention if: a child has a high fever that does not respond to paracetamol or ibuprofen; the child is under three months with any fever; there is difficulty breathing; the child is unusually pale, limp or unresponsive; there is a rash or persistent vomiting; the illness is lasting longer than expected; or the parent's instinct tells them something is seriously wrong. Persistent or severe symptoms that do not match a known mild illness should always be checked by a healthcare professional.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The UK Childhood Immunisation Schedule</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">The UK routine childhood immunisation schedule is free on the NHS and follows a nationally agreed timetable updated from January 2026. At 8 weeks: 6-in-1 vaccine (diphtheria, tetanus, whooping cough, polio, Hib, hepatitis B — first dose), Rotavirus (oral, first dose), MenB (first dose). At 12 weeks: 6-in-1 (second dose), MenB (second dose), Rotavirus (second dose). At 16 weeks: 6-in-1 (third dose), PCV (pneumococcal — first dose). At 1 year (12–13 months): MMRV vaccine (measles, mumps, rubella and varicella — first dose), PCV booster, MenB booster. Note: from January 2026 the MMRV vaccine replaces MMR; the Hib/MenC booster has been discontinued for children born from July 2024. At 18 months (new appointment, for children born on or after 1 July 2024): 6-in-1 (fourth dose), MMRV (second dose). From 2 years, annually each autumn: flu vaccine (nasal spray) — the programme continues through primary and secondary school age. At 3 years 4 months: 4-in-1 pre-school booster (diphtheria, tetanus, whooping cough, polio). At 12–13 years (girls and boys): HPV vaccine. At 14 years: 3-in-1 teenage booster (diphtheria, tetanus, polio), MenACWY. This schedule protects children from serious diseases at the age they are most vulnerable.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n9" data-revision-tip="For exam questions on immunisation, practise stating the difference between herd immunity and individual protection. A question on 'benefits of immunisation' expects both — don't write only about protecting the individual child.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Herd immunity is achieved when a high enough proportion of the population (typically 90–95% for measles) is immune to a disease, so it cannot spread easily — protecting those who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborns and immunocompromised individuals. Maintaining high uptake rates is essential for herd immunity to hold.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Benefits and Concerns Around Immunisation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">The benefits of immunisation are well established: vaccines have eradicated smallpox globally and near-eliminated polio; MMR vaccination has dramatically reduced measles cases; herd immunity protects vulnerable groups who cannot be vaccinated. Immunisation is free on the NHS, saving families the cost of treatment for serious illness. However, some parents express concerns: minor side effects such as redness, swelling or a mild fever at the injection site are common and short-lived. More serious reactions (anaphylaxis) are extremely rare — approximately 1 in 1 million doses. A now-retracted 1998 study (Wakefield) claimed a link between the MMR vaccine and autism; this has been comprehensively discredited by large-scale studies involving millions of children, and Wakefield lost his medical licence. Despite this, vaccine hesitancy remains a public health challenge, and health visitors play a key role in providing evidence-based reassurance to parents with concerns.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n12">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n13">Common childhood illnesses include chickenpox, measles, mumps and tonsillitis; meningitis is a medical emergency identified by a non-blanching rash, stiff neck, high fever, severe headache and photophobia — call 999 if these signs appear.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n14">The UK childhood immunisation schedule offers free vaccines from 8 weeks to 14 years, protecting against diseases including MMRV, meningococcal disease, polio and HPV, and contributing to herd immunity for vulnerable groups.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n15">Immunisation's benefits (disease prevention, herd immunity, free on the NHS) far outweigh the risks; the alleged MMR–autism link has been comprehensively discredited by large-scale research.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n11">When writing about immunisation, always distinguish between individual benefits (protecting the vaccinated child) and population benefits (herd immunity protecting those who cannot be vaccinated). For meningitis questions, name specific warning signs — 'a non-blanching rash' and 'photophobia' score more marks than 'a rash' and 'light sensitivity'. On concerns around immunisation, you may acknowledge parental worries but must also state that the MMR–autism link has been comprehensively discredited.</p>

